WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.089 --> 00:00:18.040
"I've found my champion."

2
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:21.022
Hermes peered through the crack in the temple wall.

3
00:00:21.042 --> 00:00:25.125
"Finish creating the passage, my friend."

4
00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:27.267
"Are you certain?"

5
00:00:27.707 --> 00:00:28.948
Caerus sounded bored.

6
00:00:30.676 --> 00:00:33.417
"These time passages aren't a simple matter, you know.

7
00:00:33.837 --> 00:00:38.640
None of the other gods could even begin to—" "I know," Hermes interrupted.

8
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.081
"That's why I asked you to help me.

9
00:00:41.741 --> 00:00:42.141
Trust me.

10
00:00:42.661 --> 00:00:44.502
It will be worth the effort if we succeed."

11
00:00:44.942 --> 00:00:45.663
He smiled.

12
00:00:47.443 --> 00:00:50.065
Caerus smiled back, looking more interested now.

13
00:00:50.085 --> 00:00:52.105
"True," he said.

14
00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:57.608
"Zeus will never figure out where you found your champion!"

15
00:00:59.744 --> 00:01:01.145
Hermes didn't respond.

16
00:01:01.745 --> 00:01:03.506
He was watching the mortal boy again.

17
00:01:05.546 --> 00:01:10.188
It wasn't easy to see through the tiny crack in the wall—curse Caerus and his laziness!

18
00:01:11.269 --> 00:01:12.489
But Hermes could see enough.

19
00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:18.292
The athlete was handsome and strong, his muscles rippling as he ran.

20
00:01:19.432 --> 00:01:22.833
Other mortals were chasing the boy, but he outpaced them easily.

21
00:01:23.854 --> 00:01:24.994
Suddenly the boy stopped.

22
00:01:25.835 --> 00:01:29.096
He threw something on the ground and then leaped around gracefully.

23
00:01:31.072 --> 00:01:32.974
"Is he celebrating his victory in the footrace?"

24
00:01:33.474 --> 00:01:34.195
Hermes thought.

25
00:01:34.975 --> 00:01:37.157
Watching him, Hermes had no idea.

26
00:01:38.158 --> 00:01:42.401
The ways of mortals were often strange, and usually not worth puzzling over.

27
00:01:43.442 --> 00:01:46.845
But Hermes could tell that this mortal was something special.

28
00:01:46.865 --> 00:01:51.209
"Finish the passage," he told Caerus again.

29
00:01:51.229 --> 00:01:54.932
"We don't have time to waste if we want to have him ready."

30
00:02:01.772 --> 00:02:06.515
"Good game, Brody," Coach Smith said as Brody jogged off the field with the rest of the football

31
00:02:06.535 --> 00:02:09.557
team. "Yeah, I know."

32
00:02:10.098 --> 00:02:11.479
Brody puffed out his chest.

33
00:02:11.499 --> 00:02:13.120
"Thanks, Coach.

34
00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.501
Do you think we'll win the championship this year?"

35
00:02:16.622 --> 00:02:17.983
Another player glanced over.

36
00:02:18.003 --> 00:02:20.985
"If you keep making touchdowns, Brody?

37
00:02:21.305 --> 00:02:22.786
Definitely!" he said.

38
00:02:24.468 --> 00:02:28.110
Several of Brody's other teammates agreed, though some remained quiet.

39
00:02:29.271 --> 00:02:30.632
A few looked unhappy.

40
00:02:32.092 --> 00:02:33.412
Brody wasn't surprised.

41
00:02:34.653 --> 00:02:38.334
He was only a freshman, but he played better than any of the older kids.

42
00:02:39.334 --> 00:02:40.875
That had to be embarrassing for them.

43
00:02:41.635 --> 00:02:42.915
Brody didn't care, though.

44
00:02:43.936 --> 00:02:45.596
They should be glad he was on the team.

45
00:02:48.637 --> 00:02:51.838
Everyone said he was the best player to attend the high school in years.

46
00:02:53.319 --> 00:02:55.279
Maybe he was the best player ever.

47
00:02:56.379 --> 00:02:57.940
Brody headed into the locker room.

48
00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:03.441
He showered, changed his clothes, and went back outside.

49
00:03:04.882 --> 00:03:06.102
A teammate waved good-bye.

50
00:03:06.122 --> 00:03:09.824
"Are you ready for that science test tomorrow?"

51
00:03:10.224 --> 00:03:10.744
he called.

52
00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:13.166
Brody frowned at him.

53
00:03:13.186 --> 00:03:15.547
"Who cares about science?"

54
00:03:17.868 --> 00:03:20.369
The truth was, he hadn't studied for the test yet.

55
00:03:21.549 --> 00:03:25.271
Science was hard for him—even harder than most of his other classes.

56
00:03:26.772 --> 00:03:27.532
But so what?

57
00:03:27.948 --> 00:03:32.069
The coach was already saying that Brody was sure to win a college scholarship when the time came.

58
00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:38.451
Brody didn't care much about going to college, but he loved the idea of playing college football.

59
00:03:40.011 --> 00:03:44.053
After that he'd probably be drafted by the professional league, and then he'd be rich.

60
00:03:44.653 --> 00:03:47.013
He wouldn't need to know about science to play football.

61
00:03:48.034 --> 00:03:51.135
The only schooling he'd need was enough math to add up all his money!

62
00:03:52.575 --> 00:03:55.716
As Brody was thinking about that, a girl stepped in front of him.

63
00:03:56.444 --> 00:03:59.185
He almost crashed into her, but he stopped just in time.

64
00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:01.485
"Watch it," he said.

65
00:04:01.505 --> 00:04:03.406
"I'm trying to walk here."

66
00:04:03.426 --> 00:04:06.727
"I need to talk to you."

67
00:04:07.467 --> 00:04:08.727
The girl sounded annoyed.

68
00:04:10.608 --> 00:04:11.548
Brody stared at her.

69
00:04:12.288 --> 00:04:13.088
She looked familiar.

70
00:04:13.108 --> 00:04:15.869
"Hey, don't you ride my bus?"

71
00:04:15.889 --> 00:04:19.930
"Yes." The girl sounded even more annoyed now.

72
00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:22.471
"I sit in the front seat.

73
00:04:23.051 --> 00:04:25.032
Every single day since school started.

74
00:04:25.392 --> 00:04:25.992
I'm Julia."

75
00:04:27.796 --> 00:04:30.917
"Oh. Uh, I'm Brody."

76
00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:31.417
"I know."

77
00:04:32.017 --> 00:04:33.238
Julia rolled her eyes.

78
00:04:33.258 --> 00:04:37.499
"I'm supposed to write an article about you for the school newspaper."

79
00:04:39.079 --> 00:04:40.640
"Really?" Brody shrugged.

80
00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.821
"There was an article about me in there three weeks ago.

81
00:04:44.321 --> 00:04:45.621
I guess it's time for another one."

82
00:04:47.482 --> 00:04:48.582
He laughed loudly.

83
00:04:50.222 --> 00:04:51.363
Julia didn't laugh.

84
00:04:52.583 --> 00:04:55.324
"Just so you know, I don't care about football.

85
00:04:55.704 --> 00:05:00.407
They're making me do this because the reporter who usually writes the sports stories is sick."

86
00:05:01.887 --> 00:05:04.029
"Whatever." Brody started walking.

87
00:05:04.049 --> 00:05:06.350
"I'm on my way to the bus stop.

88
00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:08.131
You can interview me if you can keep up."

89
00:05:09.772 --> 00:05:12.273
Julia broke into a jog to match his pace.

90
00:05:12.293 --> 00:05:16.155
"Can't you hold still for a second?

91
00:05:16.536 --> 00:05:17.556
This won't take long."

92
00:05:18.337 --> 00:05:19.397
She looked at her notebook.

93
00:05:21.018 --> 00:05:23.780
"What's your favorite thing about playing on the football team?"

94
00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:27.021
That got Brody's interest.

95
00:05:27.781 --> 00:05:29.642
He loved talking about himself.

96
00:05:30.782 --> 00:05:33.563
He stopped and leaned against the brick wall of the gymnasium.

97
00:05:33.583 --> 00:05:36.645
"Well he said.

98
00:05:36.665 --> 00:05:40.786
"My favorite thing about playing football is that I'm so great at it.

99
00:05:41.287 --> 00:05:42.827
It's fun being the star of the team."

100
00:05:44.448 --> 00:05:46.749
He flexed his biceps like a bodybuilder.

101
00:05:46.769 --> 00:05:54.212
"Hmm." Julia didn't look impressed by that, or by his answer either, but she scribbled a few notes.

102
00:05:55.492 --> 00:05:58.774
"What valuable lessons have you learned from your teammates so far?"

103
00:05:59.974 --> 00:06:03.616
That question wasn't nearly as interesting as the first one, in Brody's opinion.

104
00:06:04.656 --> 00:06:06.637
"What kind of article are you writing, anyway?"

105
00:06:07.117 --> 00:06:07.557
he said.

106
00:06:07.577 --> 00:06:10.359
"Readers won't care about stuff like that.

107
00:06:10.799 --> 00:06:14.300
Maybe you should wait until that sick reporter gets back and let him write the story."

108
00:06:15.341 --> 00:06:16.501
He started to walk away.

109
00:06:17.382 --> 00:06:19.302
Then he felt Julia grab his arm.

110
00:06:20.463 --> 00:06:22.624
She had a strong grip for such a nerdy girl.

111
00:06:23.596 --> 00:06:25.837
"Stop," she said, sounding annoyed.

112
00:06:25.857 --> 00:06:29.898
"I told you, I need to—" Whoosh!

113
00:06:30.718 --> 00:06:33.319
A sudden blast of air and sound interrupted her.

114
00:06:34.179 --> 00:06:36.720
The bricks of the gym wall seemed to crackle and melt.

115
00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:43.062
They pulled apart, leaving a narrow opening just tall and wide enough for a person to step through.

116
00:06:44.202 --> 00:06:46.383
Brody felt Julia's grip on him tighten.

117
00:06:47.083 --> 00:06:50.344
As he looked through the crack, his eyes widened in shock.

118
00:06:51.308 --> 00:06:54.230
There was no sign of the gym interior through that crack in the wall.

119
00:06:56.092 --> 00:07:00.135
Instead he saw a strange, shadowy space with stone walls.

120
00:07:00.915 --> 00:07:02.717
Two tall figures stood there.

121
00:07:04.358 --> 00:07:06.199
One of the figures leaned through the crack.

122
00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:11.003
He had curly blond hair and looked a few years older than Brody.

123
00:07:11.724 --> 00:07:13.325
His eyes glowed bright green.

124
00:07:13.345 --> 00:07:17.068
"Hey Ow!"

125
00:07:17.789 --> 00:07:22.899
Brody yelped as the mysterious figure grabbed him by the hair and yanked him through the crack.
